B.e_Z=s(["Contraindicated in hemolytic anemia and hemochromatosis. Avoid use in GI tract inflammation. May produce constipation, dark stools (false-positive guaiac is controversial), nausea, and epigastric pain. Iron and tetracycline inhibit each other\u2019s absorption. Antacids may decrease iron absorption. Alternating-day dosing, compared to daily dosing, may provide better iron absorption for those experiencing GI side effects.","Iron preparations are variably absorbed. Less GI irritation when given with or after meals. Vitamin C, 200 mg per 30 mg iron, may enhance absorption. Liquid iron preparations may stain teeth. Give with dropper or drink through straw."],t.s)
B.bRr=new A.R("IRON\u2015ORAL PREPARATIONS","Ferrous sulfate: Fer-In-Sol, Slow FE, Slow Iron, and many generics\nFerrous gluconate: Ferate and generics\nFerrous fumarate: Ferretts, Ferrimin 150, and generics\nPolysaccharide-iron complex: EZFE 200, Poly-Iron 150, iFerex 150, NovaFerrum, NovaFerrum Pediatric Drops, and many other brands; previously available as Niferex","Oral iron supplements",B.dsh,B.e01,B.e_Z,"Pregnancy category is \u201cA\u201d for ferrous sulfate and is unknown for the other salt forms.","Harriet Lane Handbook, 24th ed. \u2014 pp. 1049\u20131050")
B.dpA=s(["Injection: 372 mg (200 mg isavuconazole) (contains 96 mg mannitol and sulfuric acid for pH adjustment); preservative free","Capsules: 74.5 mg (40 mg isavuconazole), 186 mg (100 mg isavuconazole); contains disodium EDTA"],t.s)
