B.dY8=s(["Hypoglycemia should be treated initially with IV glucose; diazoxide should be introduced only if patient is refractory to glucose infusion. Should not be used in patients hypersensitive to thiazides unless benefit outweighs risk. Thiazides may enhance diazoxide\u2019s hyperglycemic effects. Use with caution in renal impairment (clearance of drug is reduced); consider dosage reduction.","Sodium and fluid retention is common in young infants and adults and may precipitate congestive heart failure (CHF) in patients with compromised cardiac reserve (usually responsive to diuretics). Hirsutism (reversible), GI disturbances, transient loss of taste, tachycardia, ketoacidosis, palpitations, rash, headache, weakness, and hyperuricemia may occur. Pulmonary hypertension in newborns/infants treated for hypoglycemia (especially at doses \u226510 mg/kg/24 hr) has been reported, and resolution/improvement of the condition was achieved after discontinuing diazoxide. Monitor BP closely for hypotension. Diazoxide binds to albumin, which may displace bilirubin and increase the effects of warfarin.","Hyperglycemic effect with PO administration occurs within 1 hr with a duration of 8 hr."],t.s)
